{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Your son-in-law, your sons, and your daughters</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 19:12): Who else of yours is in the city besides your wife and your daughters who are in the house? If you have a son-in-law or sons and daughters, bring them out of the place. If so, their speech follows the way of people, for he had no sons but only daughters. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 19:12): 'Your son-in-law and your sons' — sons-in-law who are like your sons. And it is possible that he had grown sons married to wives, and he spoke with his sons-in-law first, for he thought his sons would listen to him; but when his sons-in-law mocked him and the matter dragged on among them, dawn rose and the angels did not let him take any but those found with him in the house. Now, the merit of Lot would have saved sons and daughters and sons-in-law — not as Abraham thought, that He would kill the righteous with the wicked. And it is clear that the angels knew the will of the Most High in this matter, for even Zoar was saved through his prayer. And it is possible that this was for the honor of the host, for it is the way of propriety for messengers to save the master of their house and all that is his, as the messengers of Joshua did (Joshua 6:23), who saved also the entire family of the mistress of the house (Genesis Rabbah 50:11) — Lot, because he honored the angel, was shown favoritism."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:32:01.479909472Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}